<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Shows the GuildhallBar menu bar icon at login.

    sh swift/build.sh --install
    cp contrib/dev.guildhall.bar.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.guildhall.bar.plist

  To stop it coming back:
    launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.guildhall.bar.plist

  **KeepAlive is deliberately false, unlike the headless service.** The app has a
  Quit item, and Quit has to mean it: with KeepAlive on, launchd would relaunch
  it a second later and the menu item would look broken. The service is the
  opposite case — nobody quits a daemon on purpose, so it should come back.

  **A LaunchAgent rather than SMAppService.** The modern API registers a login
  item from inside the app, which is nicer, and it wants a signed bundle. This app
  is built locally and not signed, so the agent is the thing that actually works.
  It also matches how the other menu bar apps on this machine are started.

  The log goes to ~/Library/Logs, not /tmp: /tmp is world-writable and
  world-readable, so session and project names would be visible to any local
  user, and a symlink pre-planted at that path would have launchd — running as
  you — truncate a file of their choosing.

  This is only the icon. The browser view is dev.guildhall.headless, and it does
  not need this to be running — quitting the icon does not stop the service.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>dev.guildhall.bar</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/Applications/GuildhallBar.app/Contents/MacOS/GuildhallBar</string>
	</array>

	<!--
		Interactive, or launchd throttles it.
		
		launchd.plist(5): "If left unspecified, the system will apply light resource
		limits to the job, throttling its CPU usage and I/O bandwidth" — and
		"Interactive jobs run with the same resource limitations as apps, that is to
		say, none. Interactive jobs are critical to maintaining a responsive user
		experience."
		
		This is a menu bar app a person clicks. It was being run as a background job
		with a throttled CPU share, which is the wrong class of job entirely.
		
		MEASURED, with proc_pid_rusage QoS accounting on the same binary:
		
		  started by this agent, no ProcessType   0.0 ms user_interactive, 4735.8 ms utility
		  started by LaunchServices (`open -a`)   1482.4 ms user_interactive, 8.9 ms utility
		  started by this agent, Interactive      9.8 ms utility
		  started by this agent, Standard         still clamped — Standard means unspecified
		
		Every bit of the app's work, main thread included, ran at `utility`. `sample`
		during a slow open found the main thread parked in __CFRunLoopRun for 4234 of
		4374 samples: not working, not scheduled. The same cold open costs about
		250 ms of CPU either way — clamped that took 528 ms of wall clock (48% CPU),
		unclamped 360 ms (72%) — and the settings switch, 115-140 ms of CPU, took
		1474 ms clamped against 127 ms unclamped on an idle machine.
		
		launchd CACHES the job definition, so editing this file changes nothing on its
		own. It has to be reloaded:
		
		  launchctl bootout   gui/$(id -u)/dev.guildhall.bar
		  launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.guildhall.bar.plist
	-->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Interactive</string>

	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<false/>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/Users/CHANGEME/Library/Logs/guildhall-bar.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/Users/CHANGEME/Library/Logs/guildhall-bar.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
